<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    PowerFramework.Shared.Diagnostics
    =================================

    Shared library: assertions, the structured assertion failure payload, stack trace capture and
    caller information. One of the six shared libraries under shared/ (Kernel, Diagnostics, Eventful,
    Localization, Containers, Contracts) that sit BENEATH the four Phase 1 services. Per AAP §0.1.5
    that layer "carries only pure behaviour and no I/O", and this project holds to it literally.

    Legacy sources ported into this folder, one C# file per legacy object (AAP §0.4.2.3):

        Assert.cs              ws_objects/pfw.common.pbl.src/assert.srf
                               The 7 assertion overloads plus the seven field failure payload, joined
                               on CRLF, that ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/pfw.sra:L111-L144 unpacks before
                               it terminates the process. Fail fast is preserved as fail fast.
        AssertionFailure.cs    ws_objects/pfw.common.pbl.src/assertionfailed.sru
        StackTraceProvider.cs  ws_objects/pfw.common.pbl.src/stacktrace.srf
                               ws_objects/pfw.common.pbl.src/stacktraceinfo.srf
        CallerInfo.cs          ws_objects/pfw.common.pbl.src/getcurrentscript.srf

    Those legacy paths are the READ ONLY behavioural oracle (AAP §0.7.3 C-C): read, never edited,
    moved or reformatted. The two PowerBuilder project objects under ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src are NOT
    translated into this build; AAP §0.8.6 R5 records that they contradict each other and that both
    reference a library absent from the repository, so neither would build as written. They are
    reference for build intent only. Nor is pfw.pbt's LibList ordering encoded here: that ordering is
    semantically significant in PowerBuilder because it resolves one flat global namespace, and
    ProjectReference has no ordering analogue.

    GOVERNING CONSTRAINTS. review_rules reports "No user rules provided", so no user rule governs
    this file and none was invented. In their place AAP §0.7.2 (the enterprise baseline) and §0.7.3
    (the twelve binding non-rule constraints) apply, and each decision below cites the constraint it
    discharges. Structural authorities: §0.3.1 names this file as the first child of this folder,
    §0.5.4.3 classifies every project file as a CREATE, §0.3.2.1 fixes the validated build mechanism,
    and §0.5.4.1 makes central package management mandatory rather than optional.

    DELIBERATELY NOT DECLARED HERE, because every one of them is inherited by walk up from the
    repository root Directory.Build.props, which is the single point of control for the whole tree:

        TargetFramework, LangVersion, Nullable, ImplicitUsings, TreatWarningsAsErrors,
        EnableNETAnalyzers, AnalysisLevel, ManagePackageVersionsCentrally, Deterministic,
        ContinuousIntegrationBuild, Product, Company, Copyright.

    Those names appear immediately above as documentation and nowhere else: not one of them is
    declared in this file. Strip the comments and what remains is three properties and two items.

    Restating any of them would be a defect even where the value happened to match, because it
    silently decouples this project from that single point of control, which is the exact drift the
    central configuration design exists to prevent. OutputType is absent for the same class of
    reason: the SDK default of Library is correct here, and only the hand authored xunit.v3 test
    projects need Exe. There are no Compile items either, because SDK style projects glob the four
    .cs files in this directory automatically. And there is no packing metadata (PackageId, Version,
    IsPackable): nothing in the AAP publishes these libraries as NuGet packages; they are consumed
    by ProjectReference.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Both values equal the directory derived defaults, and are stated anyway so that a future
         directory rename cannot silently change the namespace and assembly identity that the four
         .cs files here, the sibling test project and the consuming services all resolve against. -->
    <RootNamespace>PowerFramework.Shared.Diagnostics</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>PowerFramework.Shared.Diagnostics</AssemblyName>
    <Description>PowerFramework shared diagnostics: assertions, the structured assertion failure payload, stack trace capture and caller information. Ported from the pfw.common.pbl assert, assertionfailed, stacktrace, stacktraceinfo and getcurrentscript objects.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Project references: exactly one, and it must stay that way">
    <!--
      Constraint discharged here: AAP §0.7.3 C-I, each service builds and tests independently.

      Kernel is referenced for ONE reason. The assertion overloads guard on the return code algebra
      before they build a payload: assert.srf:L101 and :L107 call IsSucceeded(rtcode), and :L113 and
      :L119 call IsValidObject(object). Both predicates land in Kernel as Predicates.IsSucceeded and
      Predicates.IsValidObject, and the tri state boundary they carry (a prevention reads as a
      success; cancelled and null are neither) is behaviour to preserve by reuse, never to
      reimplement locally where it could drift.

      It is DELIBERATELY NOT a reference for PfwException. In the legacy hierarchy
      assertionfailed.sru:L4 declares "from runtimeerror" and pfwexception.sru:L3 declares "from
      runtimeerror" as well, so AssertionFailure is a SIBLING of PfwException and not a subclass.
      AssertionFailure must not be made to inherit from PfwException.

      C-I also bounds this list: nothing outside shared/ may be referenced, which is what keeps every
      service independently restorable, buildable and testable from a clean checkout. Relative path
      only. Never an absolute path, and never a package reference standing in for a project.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel\PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ZERO PackageReference elements, and zero is the correct count (AAP §0.7.3 C-A, §0.1.5).

    Every native binding this project replaces already has a Base Class Library substitute, so there
    is nothing to buy (AAP §0.6.5 substitution matrix):
      * stacktrace.srf:L7 binds pfwStackTrace out of the closed pfw.dll, for which no C++ source
        exists anywhere in the repository. Substitute: System.Diagnostics.StackTrace.
      * getcurrentscript.srf:L7 and :L8 bind pfwGetCurrentScript out of that same closed binary.
        Substitute: the System.Runtime.CompilerServices caller information attributes.

    Nothing in this project performs I/O, networking, logging or serialization, so no ASP.NET
    reference, no web SDK and no third party package belongs in it. If a package ever does become
    genuinely necessary, add it VERSIONLESS and pin the version in the repository root
    Directory.Packages.props. ManagePackageVersionsCentrally is enabled repository wide (AAP
    §0.5.4.1), so an inline Version attribute here is a build error rather than a shortcut, and a
    floating or wildcard version is barred outright by the §0.7.2 baseline.
  -->

  <ItemGroup Label="Test seam for the per service coverage gate">
    <!--
      Constraint discharged here: AAP §0.7.3 C-H, 80 percent line coverage per in scope service.

      Assert.cs derives its payload from the captured call stack: assert.srf:L38 selects the caller
      frame at sCallStack[nCount minus 2] and :L61 copies frames 1 through nCount minus 2 into the
      failure record. That formatting cannot be driven deterministically through the public overloads
      alone, because the frames it reads depend on whichever test method called them. Making the
      internal payload builder visible to the sibling test project is what allows the 80 percent line
      coverage gate, measured from the Cobertura report, to be met with real assertions instead of
      brittle stack shaped fixtures.

      The .NET SDK converts this MSBuild item into the assembly attribute during build, which is why
      this project deliberately contains no AssemblyInfo.cs and no Properties folder. Test visibility
      grants no behavioural latitude whatsoever: nothing about the observable assertion surface
      changes because of this item.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="PowerFramework.Shared.Diagnostics.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
